,title,link,text,language,family
0,Samo language (New Guinea) - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samo_language_(New_Guinea),"January 1, 2026 - Samo, also known as Daba, Nomad, and Supei, is a Trans–New Guinea language of New Guinea, spoken in the plains east of the Strickland River in Western Province of Papua New Guinea.",Samo,Trans-New Guinea
1,Music of Papua New Guinea - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Papua_New_Guinea,"December 18, 2025 - A dance rattle from East Sepik Province in Papua New Guinea. Rattles like these may form part of traditional dress, tied to the ankles of people dancing in traditional ‘singsings’ · Wooden pipes made of thin bamboo are used for music making and ceremonies.",Samo,Trans-New Guinea
2,Language: Samo,http://transnewguinea.org/language/samo?sort=loan,"Classification: Trans-New Guinea, East Strickland · Glottolog page for Samo · Ethnologue page for Samo · Open Language Archives search for Samo · Wikipedia page for Samo · Multitree language relationships for Samo · Glottolog resources for Samo · 1 · 2 · 3 ·",Samo,Trans-New Guinea
3,A Trans superstar of South Pacific music- I'm Moshanty,https://www.losangelesblade.com/2020/12/15/a-trans-superstar-of-south-pacific-music-im-moshanty/,"August 27, 2021 - But for veteran LGBTQ filmmaker and longtime U.S. State Department Arts Envoy, Human Rights Ambassador, Cultural Affairs expert and film documentarian Tim Wolff, a chance encounter led to the incredible story of a transgender music superstar and Papua New Guinea national hero Moses Moshanty Tau.",Samo,Trans-New Guinea
4,Samo,http://transnewguinea.org/language/samo?sort=source_gloss,"Classification: Trans-New Guinea, East Strickland · Glottolog page for Samo · Ethnologue page for Samo · Open Language Archives search for Samo · Wikipedia page for Samo · Multitree language relationships for Samo · Glottolog resources for Samo · 1 · 2 · 3 ·",Samo,Trans-New Guinea
5,About: Samo language (New Guinea),https://dbpedia.org/page/Samo_language_(New_Guinea),"Samo, also known as Daba, Nomad, and Supei, is a Trans–New Guinea language of New Guinea, spoken in the plains east of the Strickland River in Western Province of Papua New Guinea. It has switch-reference marking for the subject of a clause.",Samo,Trans-New Guinea
6,Samo - Wikidata,https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7409884,language spoken in Papua New Guinea · Samo language edit · instance of · language · 1 reference · stated in · Ethnologue · modern language · 1 reference · reference URL · https://iso639-3.sil.org/code/smq · subclass of · Papuan · 0 references · part of · Trans–New Guinea ·,Samo,Trans-New Guinea
7,Samo language (New Guinea),https://grokipedia.com/page/samo_language_new_guinea,"The Samo language, also known as Nomad, Daba, or Supei, is a Trans-New Guinea language spoken by approximately 900 people in the Lake Murray district of Western Province, Papua New Guinea. It belongs to the Bosavi branch of the Central and South New Guinea phylum and is classified as a stable indigenous language with no immediate threat of endangerment, serving as the primary means of communication within its ethnic community.",Samo,Trans-New Guinea
8,PeopleGroups.org - Samo of Papua New Guinea,https://www.peoplegroups.org/Explore/groupdetails.aspx?peid=10411,"Overview: The Samo of Papua New Guinea, numbering 1,900, are No Longer Unreached. They are part of the New Guinea people cluster within the Pacific Islanders affinity bloc. This people group is only found in Papua New Guinea. Their primary language is Samo.",Samo,Trans-New Guinea
9,Sacred Flute Music From New Guinea: Madang / Windim Mabu | Ragnar Johnson assisted by Jessica Mayer | Ideologic Organ,https://ideologicorgan.bandcamp.com/album/sacred-flute-music-from-new-guinea-madang-windim-mabu,"April 28, 2025 - SOMA024 Sacred flutes are blown (""Windim Mambu'') to make the cries of spirits by adult men in the Madang region of Papua New Guinea. Pairs of long bamboo male and female flutes are played for ceremonies in the coastal villages near the Ramu River.",Samo,Trans-New Guinea
